2|0@SandersForPresident^1440101254#cu9v65u:Lucky137$:I'm not going to touch your first two comments, but as for &gt;We need to be pushing trade school. We're in agreement. Something I posted ~ a month ago: &gt;I think this is a somewhat narrow-minded idea, and maybe putting the cart before the horse a bit... While I'm all for as much education as possible for whoever wants it, and not requiring people to leverage their future to get it, can we focus on bolstering pre-K and K-12 first? I would support doubling pay for preschool, primary, and secondary public school teachers and investing in school resources before this. **And what about reinvesting in the vocational arts? Why don't we support professions that can't easily be outsourced to other countries - auto mechanics, construction, tradeworks - that our country needs, rather than turning STEM competition into a numbers game?** &gt;Plus, take it from someone who attend one of them, large public institutions do not need - and do not have room for - more people. The three large (500+ seats) lecture rooms on my campus were fully scheduled 11 hours per day, with no empty seats. Where are we physically going to put these people? And given some of the academic achievement (or lack thereof) I have seen from my fellow students, it's clear to me that already students are coming in without the background knowledge and skills that they need to be successful in higher education, and that they should be learning from their standard education. How can we open a doorway to more people into higher education, when every other academic doorway leading up to the point has been closed or at best barely ajar? &gt;And to echo what others have said, tuition is nothing compared what you'll pay on living costs. $9,000 per year is certainly nothing to sneeze at, but if you want to live and eat near campus, you're looking at easily $2,000 a month extra. Where's the support for that? Where's the affordable housing for students who don't have the time work, and are actively encouraged by academic advisers not to work? &gt;Just my thoughts. If this was part of a comprehensive education strategy, I'm all aboard. If it was a small, sensible step like starting to reduce student loan interest or loan forgiveness all together, I'm there. This just doesn't seem sensible to me.
