1|0@SandersForPresident^1440103683#cu9wsj7:Lucky137$:&gt; proved to be ineffective Do you have a source for that? Ineffective in what sense? In creating tomorrow's workforce? In giving people the opportunity to learn? Are we talking about the value of education in terms of future economic benefit? Or in terms of the inherent benefit of learning and growing and asking questions - learning for learning's sake? I'm not trying to be rhetorical here - I think these are all important questions. Increasing funding for K-12 and/or vocational programs need not come at the expense of funding for college, nor should it. We should be decreasing military spending, closing corporate tax loopholes, and increasing taxes for the highest earners, and using **that** money to pay for these things. I think if anything there should be **more** spending for college, so I'm not sure our common ground extends past improving vocational programs. My concern is the very narrow focus on simply college education. I think it's surprisingly narrow given Bernie's very comprehensive ideas on other issues. I think that this level of spending on college, when not matched with comparable improvements to our pre-college education system and to other work training programs, is a waste of money. My main point in my post from a month ago is that there are people in college now who frankly shouldn't be. Our country has gotten obsessed with higher education, as some required path to being a functional member of society. Often times I think this is because there aren't really any other options, and simply making it easier for people to go to college is not going to improve the number of options at all, nor the prospects of the people "forced" to enroll when they shouldn't be there in the first place.
